WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.151
Structured logging makes background services understandable.

2
00:00:04.151 --> 00:00:18.401
Useful fields include ExampleJobId, ExampleWorkerName, message ID, ExampleCorrelationId, ExampleTenantId where safe, operation name, state transition, attempt count, duration, outcome, and failure category.

3
00:00:18.401 --> 00:00:27.048
These fields help operators follow work across queues, databases, services, and logs without recording raw sensitive content.

4
00:00:27.048 --> 00:00:35.349
Workers should avoid logging secrets, tokens, message bodies, file contents, personal data, and sensitive business data.

5
00:00:35.349 --> 00:00:48.354
Background services often process large inputs and sensitive records, so broad object logging can create a second copy of the data in log systems, dashboards, exports, or support channels.

6
00:00:48.354 --> 00:00:53.542
Prefer stable identifiers, safe categories, counts, and correlation values.

7
00:00:53.542 --> 00:01:09.314
Monitoring should cover health checks, readiness signals, liveness signals, queue depth, processing rate, failure rate, retry counts, dead-letter counts, oldest message age, active job duration, and downstream dependency health.

8
00:01:09.314 --> 00:01:18.031
These signals tell teams whether the worker is alive, whether it is keeping up, and whether failures are isolated or systemic.

9
00:01:18.031 --> 00:01:31.381
Audit events are important for high-impact jobs, administrative actions, exports, sensitive data processing, permission changes, cross-tenant operations, repair tasks, and manual interventions.

10
00:01:31.381 --> 00:01:45.978
An audit record should capture who or what initiated the work, which object or tenant was affected, what decision was made, when it happened, and what the outcome was, without copying unnecessary sensitive data.

11
00:01:45.978 --> 00:01:51.512
Alerting should focus on meaningful patterns rather than every isolated failure.

12
00:01:51.512 --> 00:01:54.832
A single transient error may not require a page.

13
00:01:54.832 --> 00:02:07.076
A growing queue, repeated validation failures, rising dead-letter counts, unexpected cross-tenant scope, stuck jobs, or repeated failures after deployment may deserve attention.

14
00:02:07.076 --> 00:02:10.189
Alert routing should include clear ownership.

15
00:02:10.189 --> 00:02:17.176
Incident response readiness depends on runbooks, dashboards, operational ownership, and review loops.

16
00:02:17.176 --> 00:02:29.005
Teams should know how to pause a worker, drain a queue, inspect failed jobs safely, preserve evidence, retry approved work, roll back a deployment, and communicate impact.

17
00:02:29.005 --> 00:02:37.929
After incidents and false positives, update logging, metrics, alerts, and documentation so the next response is faster and safer.
